<Doc
  title="Armor: protect the core"
  kicker="Track A — Armor the Core"
  description="Dependency direction, ports and adapters, and how to keep frameworks out of the domain in PHP, C#, Java and C++."
  path="/guide/protect-the-core"
>
  <p class="lede">
    The domain is the only part of the system that cannot be replaced. Armor is not
    about hiding it — it is about making sure nothing else can dictate its shape.
  </p>

  <h2>The dependency rule</h2>

  <p>
    Source dependencies point inwards. Nothing in an inner circle may know the name of
    anything in an outer circle: not a class, not an annotation, not a framework
    interface, not a database type.
  </p>

<pre><code>Interface  ──▶  Application  ──▶  Domain
Infrastructure ──────────────────▶  Domain</code></pre>

  <p>
    Arrows are compile-time imports. If <code>Domain/Account.php</code> imports
    <code>Doctrine\ORM\Mapping\Entity</code>, the arrow points the wrong way and the
    framework now owns your business rules.
  </p>

  <h2>Ports and adapters</h2>

  <p>
    The domain declares what it needs as a port. Infrastructure implements it as an
    adapter. The domain keeps the vocabulary; the adapter keeps the vendor.
  </p>

  <CodeTabs
    title="The same port, four languages"
    tabs={[
      { id: 'php', label: 'PHP', code: phpPorts },
      { id: 'cs', label: 'C#', code: csPorts },
      { id: 'java', label: 'Java', code: javaPorts },
      { id: 'cpp', label: 'C++', code: cppPorts },
      { id: 'ts', label: 'TypeScript', code: tsPorts },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    The C++ version uses an abstract class with a virtual destructor because the
    language has no interfaces — the shape is identical: a named contract owned by the
    domain, with no implementation detail in sight.
  </p>

  <h2>A domain entity that knows nothing</h2>

  <CodeTabs
    title="Entity and its port"
    tabs={[
      { id: 'php', label: 'Entity', code: phpEntity },
      { id: 'cs', label: 'Port', code: phpPort },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    No annotations, no base class, no active record. The entity can be constructed in
    a unit test with no database, no container and no framework boot.
  </p>

  <h2>The anti-patterns</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>What it means</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Entity extends a framework base class</td>
          <td>The framework owns lifecycle and state</td>
          <td>Plain object + adapter mapping</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Domain imports <code>EntityManager</code>, <code>DbContext</code>, <code>Session</code></td>
          <td>Persistence is in the core</td>
          <td>Repository port in the domain</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Validation annotations on domain fields</td>
          <td>Transport rules became business rules</td>
          <td>Validate in the use case or in the value object</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Domain throws HTTP exceptions</td>
          <td>The adapter leaked inwards</td>
          <td>Domain exceptions + mapping at the edge</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Make the arrow a build failure</h2>

  <p>
    A dependency rule enforced by review decays. Enforce it with a tool that fails the
    pipeline:
  </p>

  <CodeTabs
    title="PHP — deptrac"
    tabs={[{ id: 'php', label: 'deptrac.yaml', code: deptrac }]}
  />

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Language</th><th>Architecture test</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>PHP</td><td><code>deptrac</code>, PHPStan custom rules</td></tr>
        <tr><td>C#</td><td><code>NetArchTest</code>, NDepend</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Java</td><td><code>ArchUnit</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>C++</td><td>target-level dependency rules in CMake, <code>include-what-you-use</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <Note label="Migration" variant="warn">
    <p>
      You do not need to stop the world. Extract the use case first, give it ports,
      then move the ORM calls into adapters one at a time. The compiler and the
      architecture test tell you when the arrow finally points inwards.
    </p>
  </Note>

  <h2>What “armor” is not</h2>

  <p>
    It is not a four-layer folder structure with the same model copied four times. If
    the domain entity and the database entity are identical, the boundary is
    ceremonial — you moved code, not dependencies. The test is the import graph, not
    the directory tree.
  </p>
</Doc>
